--- body ---
## Context

zoteus keeps its whole search index resident in JS objects and snapshots it with
a single `JSON.stringify`. Measured on a 7 540-item Zotero library, uncapped
full text:

**The table below is the filing-time comparison and has been superseded.** It
compares different corpora at different chunk geometries; `STATE.md` carries
the like-for-like measurement (477 511 passages both sides) and the caveats
that came with it. Kept as the record of why this chantier was opened.

| | zoteus (resident JS) | FTS5 via `node:sqlite` |
|---|---|---|
| passages | 477 511 | 408 628 |
| build | 337 s | 46,6 s |
| RSS at rest | 5 370 MB | 162 MB |
| on disk | 546 MB (write fails) | 762 MB |
| reload | OOM on stock Node | opens the file |
| query | 0,37–0,5 s | 1–76 ms |

Three walls, all silent, all consequences of the same choice:

- `JSON.stringify` cannot exceed V8's max string length (536 870 888 chars).
  Past it `saveIndex` throws, `startIndexBuild` swallows it into a warning, and
  the build still reports `state: "done"` over a stale file.
- `loadIndex` OOMs at Node's ~4 GB default heap on an index that wrote fine —
  so zoteus writes an index it cannot read back. Reached around 260 000
  passages.
- `buildIncremental` calls `reset()`: a full rebuild is the only update path,
  because applying a three-item delta would mean rewriting the whole file.

Filed upstream as oscardvs/zoteus#10, with SQLite proposed there as direction
(d). No maintainer response yet. This tracker covers building it as a prototype
in the fork, without opening a merge request — the posture stated in the issue
comment.

## Blast radius

`SearchIndex` is the seam: `build.ts`, `semantic-search.ts` and every tool go
through its methods, so a SQLite-backed implementation behind the same
interface is contained. Touched: `bm25.ts` and `tokenize.ts` become FTS5 DDL
plus two queries, `persistence.ts` disappears (the database is the state),
`vector-store.ts` moves to a BLOB column, `index-manager.ts` keeps its build
bookkeeping. Roughly 300–400 lines.

## Architectural decisions to settle in 0002

Taken once, in the first child, because everything after depends on them:
schema and its indices, tokenizer configuration, where the database file lives
relative to `ZOTEUS_DATA_DIR`, and whether `SqliteSearchIndex` shadows or
replaces the existing class.

## Children

- 0002 — schema and keyword-only implementation (the only one carrying design risk)
- 0003 — build path writes rows; `persistence.ts` retires
- 0004 — vectors in a BLOB column, RRF rescoring
- 0005 — migration from an existing `search-index.json`
- 0006 — incremental update via the library version watermark
- 0007 — chunk geometry becomes configurable (filed 2026-08-21, after 0006:
  changing it changes results, so it cannot overlap the storage comparison)
- 0008 — quantize the vector column: implemented, measured, and shipped OFF by
  default. The 13x was a k=30 figure that does not survive being asked for a
  pool on a synthetic fixture. **Superseded 2026-08-22 by the real-vector
  measurement**: on 93 022 real embeddings recall is 0,884 at a 4x pool and
  0,953 at 8x, where the two-stage path is ~3,0x and ~1,7x faster than the exact
  scan. The fixture was a harder problem than real data. Shipped default
  unchanged; see 0008 for why that is now a decision rather than a verdict.
- 0009 — fold accents in `tokenize()`: **fixed 2026-08-22**, fork `50b3f15`.
  Both backends now fold, so the accented-query exception the invariant below
  carried is gone — the divergence was a defect, not the extra recall it was
  first recorded as.
- 0010 — corruption path for the database (filed 2026-08-21): none exists, and
  a naive rebuild would leave 0006's watermark claiming an empty index is current
- 0011 — peak build RSS is ~2 GB (filed 2026-08-21): 0003's "few hundred MB"
  verification line measured and not met; the at-rest claim is unaffected
- 0012 — does a Zotero re-extraction bump anything the watermark sees? (filed
  2026-08-21): an open question, one experiment from being answered
- 0013 — one dictionary is 9% of the index (filed 2026-08-21): no per-item
  ceiling; distorts BM25 corpus statistics and is 0011's cheapest suspect

Migration measured 2026-08-21 on two real indexes (153 MB and 321 MB, the
463 MB file no longer existing): peak RSS 80,7 -> 97,0 MiB while the file grew
3,05x -- strongly sublinear, not flat, and re-measured after review found the
first figures unbacked. The JSON backend it replaces went
1 896 MB -> 3 786 MB over the same two, about 11,8x the file. Startup 45 s
including the one-off migration, then 0,97 s on every later start, against
30,8 s every time for JSON. Database/JSON ratio 1,55x, consistently above the
1,34x budgeted from the synthetic fixture. Artifacts in
`bench/results/0005-migration/`. The full 463 MB case is no longer untested:
measured 2026-08-22 at 55,5 s, peak 93,2 MiB `VmHWM`, ratio 1,5388, artifact
`bench/results/0005-migration/migrate_463MB.json`.

Wave order is 0002, then 0003, then 0004 and 0005 in parallel, then 0006.
0006 depends on cheap partial writes, so it cannot go upstream alone.

## What the local API documentation settles

Read 2026-08-21 (`zotero.org/support/dev/web_api/v3/local_api`), against
decisions this chantier had taken by inference or by probing.

**0006's backend label is documented, not inferred.** The design recorded there
was that local and cloud version sequences are unrelated integers, so a
watermark must carry which backend produced it or a backend switch either skips
a delta or replays the library. The documentation states it outright: local
versions are maintained locally and have **"no relation to Web API versions"**,
incrementing per library per transaction regardless of change source. That
decision now has a citation rather than an argument.

**The three endpoints 0006 depends on are supported**: `?since=` filtering,
`Last-Modified-Version`, and `?format=versions` are all named. `/deleted` is
not in the supported list, consistent with the 404 measured on this machine --
so 0006's key-set reconcile through `?format=versions` is the documented route,
not a workaround.

**Reads need no authentication**; writes do, from Zotero 10 on, via a key from
`POST /api/local/authorize` plus a `Zotero-Server-ID` header. The warning that
matters for an MCP server: because reads are unauthenticated, anything running
locally can read the library, and the port must never be forwarded.

**Two differences worth carrying into the #10 report.** Results are
**unpaginated by default** on the local API, where the Web API pages -- zoteus
pages 100 at a time regardless, which is safe but leaves throughput on the
table for a local build. And sort order, quicksearch matching and JSON output
"may differ subtly from the Web API", which bears directly on 0001's
old-vs-new comparison: run both sides of that comparison through the *same*
backend, or the difference measured is partly the API's, not the index's.

**One capability the Web API lacks**, noted for elsewhere: the local API
executes saved searches at `<userOrGroupPrefix>/searches/<searchKey>/items`.
Nothing in this chantier uses it.

## Invariants

- Upstream's own suite is the gate: `npx vitest run`, `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`.
  This project's Python and prose rules do not apply to a TypeScript repo.
- **Same corpus in, same results out.** A storage swap is a pure refactor: it
  is validated by comparing search results between the old and new index on the
  same library, not by a green suite. Green tests pass on a refactor that
  quietly changed ranking.

  Operationalised in 0002 as top-k item-set overlap, because FTS5's `bm25()`
  and the JS BM25 cannot agree on scores. It carried one exception between
  2026-08-21 and 2026-08-22 — accented queries, where the backends diverged —
  and that exception is now closed by 0009. Worth remembering how it read at
  the time: the divergence was first recorded as FTS5 *out-recalling* the JS
  index, an improvement. Measurement on the real library showed it returning
  twenty wrong results. **An unexplained divergence is not evidence of the
  better side winning**, and this invariant is the thing that would have caught
  it earlier had the comparison been run on real data sooner.
- Nothing under `fork/` is committed here, and no `tickets/` directory is ever
  added to the fork — it would appear in any diff sent upstream.

## Exit criteria

- [x] All children closed -- 0002 to 0013, twelve of them (0008 closes with the
      merge that carries this line; see the closing note on why this ticket does
      not close in that same merge)
- [x] The whole library indexes with no character cap, writes, and reloads on a
      stock Node with no `--max-old-space-size`. Measured 2026-08-22 end to end:
      **477 512 passages** at `fulltext_max_chars: 0`, built in 371,6 s at a
      1 848,8 MiB peak, written as 949,4 MB, and reopened by a fresh server in
      **1,47 s at 124,5 MiB** with no heap flag at any point
      (`bench/results/0001-old-vs-new/uncapped_stock_node.json`). This is the
      geometry the JSON backend cannot reach at all: it holds the same 477 512
      passages in the heap at an 8 691,5 MiB peak and then fails to persist them,
      three times, with `Invalid string length`.
- [x] Old-vs-new result comparison documented in `bench/` --
      `bench/results/0001-old-vs-new/`, and run over ONE corpus rather than two
      (see below)
- [x] ~~Findings reported on oscardvs/zoteus#10~~ -- **criterion dropped by the
      author 2026-08-22.** Nothing goes upstream unless the maintainer answers.
      The measurements stand as the fork's own record.

## The comparison, finally like for like

The caveat this ticket recorded at filing -- that running the two sides through
two backends measures the API's differences as well as the index's -- is
retired, and by a stronger method than the one it asked for. Both backends read
**the same 360 811 passages**: one local-API crawl, its `search-index.json`, and
that same file migrated in place. Not two crawls that ought to agree, the same
rows.

| | resident JS | SQLite/FTS5 |
|---|---|---|
| startup to first answer | 90,87 s | **3,86 s** |
| resident after load | 5 406,1 MiB | **121,1 MiB** |
| resident after 16 queries | 5 759,6 MiB | **128,0 MiB** |
| on disk | 463,3 MiB | 712,9 MiB |

Same `VmHWM` instrument on both sides. **45x less memory, 23x faster to answer.**

This also retires a caveat `STATE.md` carried until tonight -- that no strictly
comparable at-rest pair existed. That was true of the geometries then in hand,
because the JSON backend cannot reach 477 512 passages. Both reach 360 811, so
the answer was to measure there rather than to keep apologising for the columns.

**One caveat measurement cannot remove, quantified rather than gestured at.**
RSS excludes the kernel page cache holding the database file, and the JS heap
figure has no such remainder. Charge the SQLite side the whole 712,9 MiB file --
the most pessimistic reading available -- and it is 840,9 MiB against
5 759,6 MiB, a **6,8x** win rather than 45x. Both are measured, on the same
corpus, and both belong in any external claim.

**Results agree as well as they can be expected to.** Mean jaccard 0,780 over 16
queries, minimum 0,429. Exact agreement was never the criterion -- 0002 defined
a match as top-k item-set overlap, because FTS5's `bm25()` and the JS BM25
cannot agree on scores. What the spread had to show is that no query class is
broken, and the class that *was* broken is now the clearest evidence: each
accented query and its unaccented spelling return identical answers
(`théorie`/`theorie` both 0,889, `mathématiques`/`mathematiques` both 0,429,
`probabilità`/`probabilita` both 1,000), where before the fix the accented form
scored 0,00 and returned twenty confident wrong hits.

## What this chantier cost, and what it kept getting wrong

Worth recording, because the same mistake recurred in four different costumes.

**A number measured at one operating point is not a property of the system.**
0008's 13x vector speedup was a k=30 figure that inverted when asked for a pool.
0011's "few hundred MB" was an expectation that a real build missed by 5x.
0005's migration memory looked "strongly sublinear" from two points and flat
from three. 0013's ranking effect looked negligible from four hand-picked
queries and moved half the orderings from seventy-two.

**A claim whose evidence was never captured is not a finding.** Three of this
chantier's claims survived review rounds on prose alone. When the artifacts were
finally produced, two of the three were wrong -- 0009's codepoint sweep had
twelve divergences in the direction it said it had none, and 0013's order test
had the wrong conclusion. The measurement that confirms is worth little; the
discipline is worth the two that did not.

**Guarding one instance of a defect class does not guard the class.** 0006
guards carefully against comparing unrelated version sequences across backends,
and then compared two unrelated sequences inside one backend (0012). The guard
was right and its scope was an accident of where the author had been looking.

## Closing, 2026-08-22 -- after the integration review, not with the last child

Held open deliberately for one more step. The convention for a tracking ticket
is that it closes after an **integration review** -- all children closed, then
re-read their diffs together, run the full suite, and check the exit criteria
against the result -- rather than on the bare event of the last child merging.
Those are different events, and collapsing them is how a tracker gets closed on
the strength of twelve individually-green children that were never looked at as
one thing. 0008 closes with the merge that carries this text; 0001 closes after
that review runs against the merged result.

Twelve children, all closed. The prototype does what the tracker was opened to
find out: the same library that costs the resident-JS index 5 759,6 MiB and
90,87 s to open costs SQLite/FTS5 **128,0 MiB and 3,86 s**, measured on one
corpus with one instrument. At the uncapped geometry the old backend cannot
write at all, the new one opens in **1,47 s at 124,5 MiB**.

Three walls this ticket named at filing are gone rather than mitigated. The
`JSON.stringify` ceiling does not exist for a database. The reload OOM does not
exist for a file that is opened rather than parsed. And `buildIncremental`'s
`reset()` is replaced by a real delta, which is what 0006 and then 0012 are
about.

What it cost to find out is recorded above, and the honest summary is that the
measurements were wrong more often than the code was. Four defects came out of
this chantier's own claims rather than out of the implementation: 0012's
watermark, 0009's twelve codepoints, 0013's order effect, and 0008's operating
point. Each was found by producing evidence for something already written down
as settled.

**Posture unchanged.** Nothing has been reported to oscardvs/zoteus#10 and
nothing will be unless the maintainer answers. The fork stands on its own.
